From 7c159429b0c2d2fa5217f60059443ab84ee95d96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 01:51:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Blu-ray] Say in kodi.log what the BD-J service says

The Java (BD-J) titles of a disc run in the isolated BD-J service, and libbluray's lines from there
reach Kodi's logger with the prefix "bdj-host:" (at most 100 a second; the client in libbluray
drops the rest and says how many). They were logged at LOGDEBUG, like every other line of
libbluray, so without debug logging nothing told why the Java of a disc stopped or waited. With a
disc whose second opening never asked for its video (issue #1), the log said only that the
demuxer found no data, and the reason could not be read.

The service takes Kodi's debug mask (DBG_CRIT | DBG_BLURAY | DBG_NAV), and libbluray's Java
reaches the log only on its critical path (errors, and what the Java of libbluray does not
implement), so these are a few lines per disc: they now go to LOGINFO, as the lines of bdj.c
already did.
---
 xbmc/filesystem/BlurayCallback.cpp | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/filesystem/BlurayCallback.cpp b/xbmc/filesystem/BlurayCallback.cpp
index a69c104..4d6c2cf 100644
--- a/xbmc/filesystem/BlurayCallback.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/filesystem/BlurayCallback.cpp
@@ -84,8 +84,12 @@ void CBlurayCallback::bluray_logger(const char* msg)
   // of bdj.c, and with the mask that Kodi sets (DBG_CRIT | DBG_BLURAY | DBG_NAV) only the
   // critical ones of that file reach here: a few per disc. They answer "why is there no menu",
   // so they go to the log without debug logging.
-  // BD-Live: each name and connection, allowed or refused (libbluray and the BD-J service)
+  // BD-Live: each name and connection, allowed or refused (libbluray and the BD-J service).
+  // The BD-J service: its lines (bdj-host:) are libbluray's critical ones there (the service takes
+  // Kodi's debug mask), at most 100 a second (its client in libbluray drops the rest and says how
+  // many); they are what says why the Java of a disc stopped or waits, and nothing else does.
   const bool bdj = msg && (std::string_view(msg).starts_with("bdj.c:") ||
+                           std::string_view(msg).starts_with("bdj-host:") ||
                            std::string_view(msg).find("BD-Live: ") != std::string_view::npos);
   CLog::Log(bdj ? LOGINFO : LOGDEBUG, "CBlurayCallback::Logger - {}", msg);
 }
